# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Change

**`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 5+.** `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. You must replace the abort-based cancellation pattern entirely.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cancellation

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var waitTime = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != null)
        {
            if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(waitTime)) != _workerTask)
            {
                // Task didn't finish in time — log warning, but you CANNOT force-kill it
                Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort` equivalent. If `GetNextItem()` blocks indefinitely without accepting a `CancellationToken`, you're stuck. You *must* make blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile` flag can't interrupt a blocked call (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take()`). Pass the token to the blocking API. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` never fires** | Any catch/logic depending on it is dead code in .NET 8. Replace with `OperationCanceledException`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` throws** | It's a `PlatformNotSupportedException` — remove entirely. |
| **Sync-over-async risk** | If you must keep synchronous `ProcessLoop`, use `_cts.Token.WaitHandle` or `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at yield points instead of `async`. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Cancellable

Worst-case escape hatch (not recommended):

```csharp
// Run in a dedicated thread that you simply abandon on timeout
_workerThread = new Thread(ProcessLoop) { IsBackground = true };
_workerThread.Start();

// On stop: set cancellation, then let the process exit kill the background thread.
// There is NO safe way to force-stop a thread in .NET 8.
```

Setting `IsBackground = true` means the thread dies on process exit, but you cannot interrupt it mid-operation otherwise.

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread` + `volatile bool` → `Task` + `CancellationTokenSource`
2. Delete all `Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.ResetAbort()` calls
3. Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
4. Pass `CancellationToken` into every blocking/async call
5. Replace `Thread.Sleep` → `await Task.Delay(ms, token)`
6. Add `IDisposable` to clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`
